            NMDOJ’S STATEMENT OF POSITION IN LIEU OF TESTIMONY

       Pursuant to the Hearing Examiners’ Order revising Procedural Deadlines issued June 18,

2026, New Mexico Department of Justice (NMDOJ) respectfully submits this Statement of

Position in Lieu of Testimony regarding the Comprehensive Stipulation of parties filed on June

22, 2026 (“Stipulation”) in this case. In support thereof, NMDOJ respectfully asserts the following.

       1.      NMDOJ participated in settlement negotiations among the parties in this case.

NMDOJ found the negotiations to be conducted in good faith, and led to a productive, reasonable

result, and a sound recommendation for the Commission. This Stipulation took into account

Southwestern Public Service’s (SPS’) needs to meet its growing obligations driven by economic

development in southeastern New Mexico, as well as the increased planning-reserve margin

requirements of the Southwest Power Pool, particularly in winter. The Stipulation also takes into

account the needs of ratepayers in the region, and the need to avoid “rate shock.” The stipulated

revenue deficiency that resulted from these negotiations, $90 million, is much closer to the $47

million revenue deficiency proposed by PRC Staff’s witnesses than it is to the $175 million
 revenue deficiency originally proposed by SPS. This is thus a favorable compromise for New

Mexico ratepayers.

       2.      Staff currently argues that “residential and small commercial customers should not

bear rate increases that are greater than the average increase.” Staff’s Statement in Opposition to

Stipulation (filed June 29, 2026, at 5). However, this argument contradicts the direct testimony of

its witness, Larry Blank. In his testimony, Dr. Blank proposed increases for Residential,

Residential Heating, and Small General of 152%, 188%, and 184% of the system average,

respectively. Direct Testimony of Larry Blank at 16-17. Conversely, the stipulation provides for

140% of the average for all three of these rate classes. While the Stipulation results in a greater

absolute increase for these rate classes than does Staff’s proposal, in line with a reasonable

compromise as to total revenue deficiency, it allows for a smaller relative increase for these classes.

       Wherefore, NMDOJ prays for the Commission to adopt orders necessary to enact the terms

of the Stipulation, and to grant all other further relief as this Commission deems just and proper.




